Designing DB

.its creating a detailed data model of a DB
.involves defining structure, storage, and retrieval mechanisms of the data 
    .to ensure it meets the needs of the users and applications that will interact with it 
.well-designed DB allows for efficient data mgt, retrieval, and storage, ensuring data integrity and security 


Need 
.efficiency - efficient storage, fast retrieval, enhanced overall performance 
.scalability - easier to scale as the data grows/ change in application requirements 
.data integrity - data remains accurate and consistent throughout its lifecycle 
.maintenance - simplified maintenance tasks like updates, backups, thus reducing risk of errors and downtime 


Process
.Requirements and analysis 
    .gather requirements - from stakeholders 
        .know what data, how it will be used, and any constraints 
    .identifying entities and relationships 
        .are objects/concepts to be stored & their interactions e.g customer placing an order 

.Conceptual design 
    .entity-relationship diagrams (ERD)
        .visual representations of entities and relationships within the DB
        .help organize and clarify the structure of the DB in a clear manner
    .identifying attributes 
        .are specific pieces of information that need to be stored about each entity e.g customer name 
    .defining relationships 
        .specify how entities are related to each other e.g one-to-one, one-to-many
        .specify any constraints 

Logical design 
    .converting ERD to tables 
        .ERD is translated into a set of tables 
        .each entity becomes a table, and each attribute becomes a column within that table 
    .normalization 
        .its organizing data to minimize redundancy and improve data integrity 
        .normal forms:
            .1NF: first normal form 
                .ensures that each column contains atomic (indivisible) values and that each column contains only one type of data 
            .2NF: second normal form 
                .build on 1NF by ensuring all non-key attributes are fully functionally dependent on the primary key
                